Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  01 REMOVED_MONTH 00 ACCESSIONED ON  01 REMOVED_MONTH 00 at 12 42 CLINICAL DATA  Suspicious mass of left breast  Please check margins                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  SEE TABLE 1 TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS   1 INVASIVE CARCINOMA Tumor size  2 X 1 X 0 8cm  gross measurement  Grade  2 Lymphatic vessel invasion  Not identified  Blood vessel invasion  Not identified  Margin of invasive carcinoma  Positive along a broad front at the margin of the specimen  Stains for receptors  Requested Diagnosis by  John H  Eichhorn  M D  Signed On  06 REMOVED_MONTH 00 at 15 36                                                                                     BREAST CANCER IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left  block B   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  The tumor cells show strong  3   overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  Grace T  McKee MD Signed On  08 REMOVED_MONTH 00 at 16 42 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received in formalin   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left breast   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm portion of yellow fibrofatty tissue which is blue inked and sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 8cm well delineated tan white  firm nodule  which comes to within 0 1cm of the nearestmargin  The remaining parenchyma is dense white fibrous tissue admixed with yellow lobulated fatty tissue  Representative sections are submitted as follows  A D  The nodule in its entirety  E G  Representative sections  Gross Description by  LORI MARINI Resident  ARASH RADFAR  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  Charles Mason Ferguson  M D  PART   BREAST  LEFT  EXCISIONAL BIOPSY   report_end  , No.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery Unit MEEI MRN  REMOVED_DATE Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 3 Results To  KEVIN S  HUGHES  M D  Signed Out by  Ronald Balassanian  M D  CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 49 year old woman with calcifications in the right breast undergoing needle localization excision  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  A  BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISION 1  SEVERE ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA  ADH  WITH ASSOCIATED CALCIFICATIONS  2  LOBULAR NEOPLASIA  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU   3  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGE WITH SCLEROSING ADENOSIS AND ASSOCIATED CALCIFICATIONS  SEE NOTE  Note  This case is challenging with a borderline or intermediate lesion  Sections reveal a 0 4cm cluster of ducts  mostly on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   which are distended by a proliferation of monomorphic atypical ductal cells  The atypical cells are enlarged with a moderate amount of cytoplasm and have enlarged nuclei with irregular nuclear contours  hyperchromatic vesicular chromatin and prominent nucleoli  The cytologic findings are suggestive of a diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in situ  DCIS   however  the area involved lacks the atypical architectural features of DCIS such as cribriform or bridging arrangement of ductal cells  Immunohistochemical stains for cytokeratin REMOVED_DATE and cytokeratin 14 were performed on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and show decreased staining of luminal ductal cells supporting the diagnosis  Although some pathologists might classify this lesion as DCIS  we feel the lesion does not meet our criteria to warrant such a diagnosis and we interpret the findings as severe atypical ductal hyperplasia  Notably  the area of ADH is present at the inferior margin of resection  Given the borderline nature of the lesion  consideration for excision of this margin is suggested  if clinically indicated  In addition to the area of ADH  foci of lobular carcinoma in situ are also present  An immunostain for e cadherin performed on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  shows lack of staining in areas of LCIS and supports the diagnosis  Dr  Melinda Lerwill and Dr  Fritz Koerner have reviewed this case and concur with the diagnosis  Dr  Kevin Hughes was notified of the diagnosis on REMOVED_DATE  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN and  right breast biopsy   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm tan yellow fragment of fibroadipose tissue  received with a needle localization wire in place and accompanied by a radiograph  The specimen is received with a short stitch denoting the anterior aspect  a long stitch denoting the lateral aspect  and a bowtie stitch denoting the cephalad aspect  as per the surgeon  The specimen is inked for orientation as follows  Anterior   blue  posterior   black  superior   green  inferior   yellow  Serial sectioning from lateral to medial reveals dense white tan rubbery fibrous tissue  with scant areas of yellow lobulated adipose tissue throughout  No definitive masses are identified grossly  Specimen is entirely submitted sequentially from lateral to medial as follows   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Lateral end   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Body   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Medial end  Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on Tuesday August 18   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 11 50 54AM By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  PART   Breast  Right   Excision  report_end  , No.
