Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 07  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 07  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery Unit Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  MICHELLE C SPECHT  M D  Consulting Pathologist s   Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Judith A Ferry  M D  Signed Out by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  CLINICAL DATA  Lymph node with atypical lymphoid infiltrate on core biopsy FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  INTRAMAMMARY LYMPH NODE WITH REACTIVE HYPERPLASIA  HEALING BIOPSY SITE  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The lymph node is slightly enlarged and shows preservation of the normal architecture  The cortex contains reactive follicles  The interfollicular area contains small lymphocytes and scattered histiocytes including some that appear activated and have vesicular nuclei  distinct nucleoli and abundant finely granular cytoplasm  There is prominent fibrous thickening of the capsule  Immunohistochemistry reveals that B cells   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    predominate in the follicles and T cells   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   cells  predominate in the interfollicular areas  A subset of mantle zone lymphocytes express dim  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   A stain for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  shows dendritic staining in follicle centers and stains mantle zone lymphocytes  Lymphoid cells are negative for cyclin  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  staining is negative within the germinal center B cells  In summary  the immunohistochemical stains reveal an atypical lymphoid population  but do not support the diagnosis of lymphoma  If the patient develops lymphadenopathy or peripheral lymphocytosis  further testing may be pursued  Dr  J  Ferry has reviewed the case and concurs with the interpretation By his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally revi