ons in the nucleophosmin gene   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   are usually small  insertions  typically 4 bp  sometimes up to 11 bp  and usually heterozygous  in  the coding region of the terminal exon 12  occurring in about 50 60  of  cytogenetically normal AMLs or about 20 25  of all AMLs  A number of studies  have evaluated the association of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutations and prognosis in AML and  demonstrated statistically significant favorable disease outcome particularly  in patients carrying the mutation but lacking  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutations  1  5  6  7  8   9       A multiplex  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  exon 12 PCR assay to detect the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   insertional mutations based on size was performed with genomic DNA samples from  blood or bone marrow  The analytical sensitivity of detecting these mutations  has been established in our laboratory at approximately 5  allele frequency or  10  mutant cells with heterozygous  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ITD or  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  insertional mutations in a  background of 90  normal cells     We have developed a single base extension assay  SNaPshot  to genotype four  bases in a portion of exon 20 from the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  gene which encode codons  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   c  REMOVED_CASE_ID G  c  REMOVED_CASE_ID A  c  REMOVED_CASE_ID T  and c  REMOVED_CASE_ID C   RefSeq NM_ REMOVED_CASE_ID  10    Genotyping is achieved in either the forward or reverse directions by single  base extension  Testing is performed on genomic DNA samples from blood or bone  marrow  The analytical sensitivity of detecting the mutation has been  established in our laboratory at approximately 1  mutant allele frequency or 2   heterozygous mutant cells for the most common c  REMOVED_CASE_ID G T mutation  Our assay  should pick up all point mutations in codon 835  GAT  and common mutations in  codon 836  e g   c  REMOVED_CASE_ID C G  c  REMOVED_