Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 2 Results To  MICHELLE C SPECHT  M D  Signed Out by  Elena F Brachtel  M D             Addended Report             Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results CLINICAL DATA  63 year old female with history of left breast cancer  status post mastectomy in  REMOVED_CASE_ID   Abnormal mammogram on right  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISION  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  CRIBRIFORM AND SOLID TYPE  SCLEROSING ADENOSIS  ATROPHY AND RADIAL SCARS  SEE TABLE  SEE NOTE  TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU TUMOR SIZE  Scattered microscopic foci  involving 4 of 9 blocks  spanning at least 1 6cm  GRADE  1  CALCIFICATIONS  Scant microcalcifications seen with benign changes  MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Ductal carcinoma in situ extends to less than 0 1cm from the anterior  posterior  superior  and inferior margins of the specimen along a broad front  STAINS FOR RECEPTORS  Requested on block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   NOTE  The pattern of sclerosing adenosis with dispersed glands  often trapped in elastotic stromal fibrosis  and superimposed atrophic changes in the vicinity of the ductal carcinoma in situ is very concerning for the presence of invasive carcinoma  Cytokeratin immunostains highlight the dispersed glandular formations  Definitive invasion  however  could not be demonstrated by myoepithelial stains  p63  calponin  myosin  blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    with the caveat that the evaluation of myoepithelial markers was limited by poor tissue preservation  CK REMOVED_DATE stains were also evaluated  Dr  F  C  Koerner has reviewed selected slides and concurs with the diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in situ  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh and  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN and  right breast biopsy with needle localization  long stitch lateral  short stitch superior   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 9cm fragment of breast tissue with a short stitch designating superior and a long stitch designating lateral  The needle localization wire is in place  The specimen is inked as follows  Anterior blue  posterior black  superior green and inferior yellow  The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral resection margin  The breast parenchyma consists of admixed fatty tissue and dense pink white fibrous stroma  An obvious tumor is not identified  Submitted entirely from medial to lateral resection margins in cassettes  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   cassette  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  representing the medial end margin and cassette  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  representing the lateral end margin  and cassettes  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  representing the area designated by needle localization wire  Final Diagnosis by Elena F Brachtel M D   Electronically signed on Wednesday May 27   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 01 57 12PM By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON   1 QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Right  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation  The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows    The tumor cells stain    10  of nuclei stained    Some tumor cells stain  1 to  10  of nuclei stained    Rare tumor cells stain    1  of nuclei stained    The tumor cells do not stain  0  of nuclei stained  These tests were develo