Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Massachusetts General Hospital  55 Fruit Street  Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID  Tel  617  REMOVED_DATE    Surgical Pathology Report      Accession     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  R   DOB  REMOVED_DATE  Age  86   Sex  F  MRN   REMOVED_CASE_ID   Location   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   LUNDER 10  Institution  MGH  Date of Operation  REMOVED_DATE  Date of Accession  REMOVED_DATE  Reported  REMOVED_DATE 14 39  Results To   Steven Lawrence McAfee MD      FINAL PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS   A  BONE MARROW  LEFT ILIAC CREST BIOPSY  ASPIRATE AND PERIPHERAL SMEAR   Hypercellular marrow with multilineage dysplasia and increased blasts consistent  with myelodysplastic syndrome  best classified as refractory anemia with excess  blasts 2   See note      Note  The bone marrow biopsy specimen is small  consists predominantly of  cortical bone and periosteum  with scant evaluable marrow  but is adequate for  evaluation  The bone marrow clot specimen contains adequate marrow particles   The marrow cellularity is overall approximately 70   range 50 to 85   The  myeloid to erythroid ratio is increased  Myeloid maturation is complete but  shifted to the left with a paucity of mature forms  Erythroid maturation is  complete but shifted to the left  Megakaryocytes are slightly increased and  include frequent micromegakaryocytes and small forms with hypolobated nuclei and  separated nuclear lobes  Primitive cells consistent with blasts occur as  scattered cells and in small clusters  The blasts are medium sized to large with  irregular to folded nuclei  distinct nucleoli  dispersed chromatin and scant  cytoplasm  No lymphoid aggregates are seen  Giemsa stain was examined as part of  the histologic evaluation of this case  Immunohistochemical stains of the core  biopsy reveal  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   blasts making up approximately 5  of the total cellularity   There is a decreased number of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   immature erythroids  in scattered clusters    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  stains approximately 10  myeloid precursors and a small number of early  erythroids  similarly to  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  highlights a slightly increased number of  megakaryocytes including many small hypolobated dysplastic forms     The aspirate smear is adequate for evaluation but hemodilute and paucicellular   The touch prep slide is adequate for evaluation  A 200 cell count reveals  57   neutrophils and precursors  12  erythroid precursors  3  lymphocytes  11 5   monocytes  6  eosinophils  2  promyelocytes  6 5  blasts  and 2  plasma cells   Myeloid maturation is left shifted and moderately dysplastic with abnormal  nuclear lobation and hypogranulation of maturing forms  Erythroid maturation is  left shifted with occasional nuclear irregularities  Megakaryocytes are rare and  include occasional small forms  The blasts are large with avoid to folded  nuclei  fine chromatic  prominent nucleoli  and scant to a moderate amount of  basophilic cytoplasm with occasional vacuolization  Eosinophils include mature  and immature forms containing eosinophilic and few basophilic granules     CBC results from REMOVED_DATE are as follows  WBC REMOVED_DATE  HGB REMOVED_DATE  HCT 30 2   MCV 100  fL  PLT 59  Manual differential  61  polys  5 7  bands  13  lymphs  13 8  monos   2 4  basos  0 8  metamyelocytes  3 3  blasts  0 nRBC 100 WBC  Review of the  peripheral smear reveals thrombocytopenia and anemia  Myeloids include  left shifted forms and occasional large primitive cells with fine chromatin   prominent nucleoli  and scant basophilic cytoplasm consistent with blasts which  were over 5  by a peripheral blood flow cytometric analysis and on a CBC from  8 27 15  The neutrophils include frequent dysplastic hypogranular forms with  hypolobation including pseudo Pelger Huet cells     Summary  Flow cytometric analysis performed on both the bone marrow aspirate   see below  and a peripheral blood specimen   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   shows about 6   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   blasts  Immunohistochemical stains on the bone marrow core biopsy and a  bone marrow aspirate count also demonstrate 5 10  myeloid blasts  These  findings along with dysplastic megakaryocytes and myeloid elements are  consistent with a diagnosis of myelodysplastic syndrome  but classified as  refractory anemia with excess blasts 2  given that the blast percentage in the  peripheral blood exceeds 5      Dr  Robert Hasserjian reviewed select slides and concurs with this  interpretation            Electronically Signed Out By Valentina Nardi MD       Resident Pathologist   Jonathan S England MD      By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis  certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or  corrected the diagnosis     PROCEDURES ADDENDA    Flow Cytometry  Ordered Date  REMOVED_DATE    SPECIMEN  Bone Marrow     DIFFERENTIAL  Lymphocytes  8   Monocytes  1   Granulocytes  69   Blasts 6      GATE ANALYZED  Lymphocyte   Blast   Monocyte    IMMUNOPHENOTYPIC POPULATIONS    OF ALL CELLS    6  Myeloid blasts   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   MPO     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  HLA DR   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID c      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID bdim     1  Polyclonal B cells   7   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   T cells with no immunophenotypic abnormalities noted   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ratio of  1 1     1  NK cells     INTERPRETATION   Myeloid blasts are increased   5  of all cells   There is no evidence for a  monoclonal B cell or unusual T cell population     Flow cytometric assessment of the following antigens was incorporated into the  evaluation and interpretation of this specimen   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESS