_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID T Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID T Wild Type    NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    34G Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    35G Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    37G Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    38G Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    181C Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    182A Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    183A Wild Type     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    263G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID G Mutant Glu542Lys   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID A Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID A Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID G Wild Type    PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    388C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    517C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    697C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID fs   800delA Wild Type     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    524G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    733G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    742C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    743G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    817C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    818G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    916C Wild Type    INTERPRETATION     POSITIVE for mutation s  in   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     This test does not rule out mutations in one of the assayed genes which might  occur at a low prevalence     TEST INFORMATION   In recent years  major efforts to define the molecular causes of cancer have  revealed a wide number of genetic aberrations  A small subset of these defects   usually referred to as  drivers   is frequently present across cancer types and  appears to be essential for oncogenesis and tumor progression  New drugs  developed to selectively target such cancer promoting pathways  are starting to  complement the more conventional therapeutic approaches  While the clinical  benefit observed with some targeted agents is encouraging  it has become clear  that for such strategies to be successful  it will be necessary to identify the  patient population carrying the genetic abnormalities targeted by each drug     We have developed a multiplexed allele specific assay to detect somatic  mutations in tumor DNA extracted from formalin fixed paraffin embedded samples   Dias Santagata  REMOVED_CASE_ID    The assay SNaPshot Version 3 detects mutations in 68  different loci from 14 cancer genes  Briefly  multiplex PCR using tumor DNA is  followed by mutation detection using single base extension sequencing technology   SNaPshot  Applied Biosystems   In addition  a separate PCR reaction using  primers flanking EGFR exon 19  EGFR exon 20 and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  exon 20 are used to detect  in frame activating insertions or deletions in EGFR and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  that may not be  detected using SNaPshot  The mutations are visualized using an ABI  REMOVED_CASE_ID  DNA  analyzer  The average analytic sensitivity of detecting mutations in this assay  has been established in our laboratory at approximately 5  mutant allele     REFERENCES   Dias Santagata D  Akhavanfard S  David SS  Vernovsky K  Kuhlmann G  Boisvert SL   Stubbs H  McDermott U  Settleman J  Kwak EL  Clark JW  Isakoff SJ  Sequist LV   Engelman JA  Lynch TJ  Haber DA  Louis DN  Ellisen LW  Borger DR  Iafrate AJ    REMOVED_CASE_ID   Rapid targeted mutational analysis of human tumours  a clinical platform  to guide personalized cancer medicine  EMBO Mol Med  2 5  146 58     This test was developed  and its performance characteristics were determined by  the MGH Diagnostic Molecular Pathology Laboratory  It has not been approved by  the U S  Food and Drug Administration  The FDA has determined that such  clearance is not necessary  This test is used for clinical purposes  Pursuant to  the requirements of CLIA 88  this laboratory has established and verified the  test accuracy and precision     Question concerning these values should be addressed to Dr  A  John Iafrate at   617 726  REMOVED_CASE_ID   MGH internal address  MGH Diagnostic Molecular Pathology  Laboratory  Massachusetts General Hospital  70 Blossom Street  GRJ  REMOVED_CASE_ID   Boston   MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID             Electronically Signed Out By  Anthony John Iafrate MD         PART   Molecular test for SNAPSHOT       report_end  , No.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Specimen Type  Consult material  Procedure Date   Ordering Provider  JAY HARRIS M D   CASE  BS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME   Resident  Mariana A  Stoleru  M D   Pathologist  Susan Lester  M D   Ph D   PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS   CONSULT SLIDES FROM MASSACHUSETTS GENERAL HOSPITAL  BOSTON  MA   RIGHT BREAST  TWO WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISIONS  ADDITIONAL MARGINS  AND AXILLARY  NODE DISSECTION   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   12 04 08    POSTERIOR  DOUBLE WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS WITH TWO CLIPS AND  CALCIFICATIONS AND TWO CLIPS   INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  moderately differentiated  modified Bloom Richardson  grade II III   measuring at least REMOVED_DATE  but extensively present at the  unoriented margin s   see additional margins and note below    Lymphovascular invasion is present   DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  solid and cribriform types  intermediate nuclear  grade   without necrosis or calcifications  is present associated with and away  from the invasive carcinoma  4 of 7 blocks  EIC positive    DCIS is   0 1cm from the unoriented margin in one focus   Special studies were performed at an outside institution with the following  results  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    not reviewed    ESTROGEN RECEPTOR POSITIVE  PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR POSITIVE   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  NEU NEGATIVE  not scored   ANTERIOR  UPPER OUTER  SINGLE WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS   Atypical ductal hyperplasia  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     Atypical lobular hyperplasia  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     Cysts with calcifications  calcium oxalate    Core needle biopsy site changes are present   FINAL LATERAL MARGIN   Columnar cell change with atypia   Note  There is a small    0 1cm   focus of atypical cells  This area is 0 2cm  from the final margin  The focus cannot be evaluated further due to the small  size and crush artifact   FINAL INFERIOR MARGIN   INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  0 3cm  is present   0 1cm from the new final  anterior margin  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     No in situ carcinoma is seen   Note  Some of the carcinoma may be present in lymphatic spaces   FINAL MEDIAL MARGIN   Benign breast tissue with no carcinoma seen   FINAL DEEP MARGIN   INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  0 5cm  is present   No lymphovascular invasion is seen   No in situ carcinoma is seen   The new final deep margin is not oriented and can not be assessed   FINAL ANTERIOR MARGIN   INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  is present as at least two foci 0 1cm and 0 2cm on  the glass slide   No lymphovascular invasion is seen   No in situ carcinoma is seen   The new final anterior margin is not oriented and can not be assessed   AXILLARY NODE DISSECTION   ISOLATED TUMOR CELLS are present in one  1  of twelve  12  axillary lymph nodes   approximately 50 cells   0 02cm    The tumor cells are not associated with a  fibrous scar  The remaining nodes do not show evidence of prior tumor  involvement   Note  The patient presented with a palpable mass  Imaging studies showed a 3  cm mass in the upper outer breast and a 1 5cm mass  3cm inferior and anterior  to the first mass  Numerous calcifications were present in the same region   There was an enlarged axillary lymph node   A core needle biopsy of the 3cm mass revealed a moderately differentiated  invasive ductal carcinoma  positive for ER and PR and negative for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  neu   1     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    A core needle biopsy performed at the same time for upper  outer calcifications did not remove the targeted calcifications and only showed  benign tissue  A fine needle aspiration of the enlarged lymph node showed  malignant cells consistent with breast cancer   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     The patient received neoadjuvant therapy  The 3cm mass decreased in size to 2  cm and the 1 5cm mass decreased in size to 1cm  The lymph node also became  smaller   The double wire localized excision removed the larger cancer marked by two  clips  Approximately half the cancer shows diminished cellularity  consistent  with a minimal response to treatment  The overall cellularity of the carcinoma  is estimated at 50   for a Miller Payne score of 2 and a Residual Cancer Burden  score of REMOVED_DATE  RCB II   The carcinoma appears to be have been transected  due to the presence of  carcinoma in three of the margins   The singe wire localized excision removed the calcifications in the  upper outer quadrant originally targeted by the prior core needle biopsy   No carcinoma is seen in this area   It is not clear that the 1 5cm mass was removed in this procedure  An  MRI after this surgery revealed a 1 9cm mass adjacent to the seroma cavity  suspicious for carcinoma  Clinical correlation is suggested   The single lymph node with metastatic carcinoma does not show evidence  of prior more extensive tumor involvement or evidence of the prior FNA   Although this could be consistent with a marked response of the metastatic  carcinoma to treatment  it is also possible that the lymph node with  metastatic carcinoma was not removed  It is unusual for metastatic  carcinoma to respond to a greater extent than the carcinoma in the breast   The post treatment MRI showed a 1cm mass in the axilla suspicious for a  lymph node  Clinical correlation is suggested to determine if the  previously involved lymph node was excised   The carcinoma would be classified as  at least  AJCC  6th edition    yp  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   i     CLINICAL DATA   History  Breast cancer  s p neoadjuvant therapy   TISSUE SUBMITTED   Consult slides   GROSS DESCRIPTION   Received from Massachusetts General Hospital  Fruit Street  Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID    are sixty three H E stained glass slides labeled   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and sublabeled    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   to   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   to   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   to   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      REMOVED_ACCESSION_